course: dictionary
lesson: 6952
title: Words for grand places
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A palace is a very large, grand house. A king, queen, president or bishop may live there. In «The Butterfly boasts that if he stamps his foot Suleiman's palace will vanish», the word palace tells you this is a huge, splendid building, not an ordinary house.
explain: Palace can also mean a big, splendid building for public fun, like a picture palace or an ice palace. Look at the clues around the word. Is someone living there, or are people visiting to enjoy themselves?
explain: Be careful with «Your new house is a palace!» Here palace is informal. It means the house is very fine, not that a king lives in it. Always swap your meaning into the sentence to check that it still makes sense.
fact: A palace is a very large, grand house where a king, queen, president or bishop lives.
fact: «Palace» can also mean a big, splendid building for public fun, such as a picture palace.
fact: In the news, «the Palace» can mean the royal family's office, as in «official statements from the Palace».
fact: Phrases to know: Buckingham Palace and royal palace.
fact: Informal use: «Your new house is a palace!» means your house is very fine.
exercise: «The Butterfly boasted that Suleiman's palace would vanish.» What is a palace? | A cold, dark cave | A small tent for camping | A shop that sells shoes | *A very large, grand house where a king or ruler lives
exercise: Priya says, «Your new house is a palace!» What does she mean? | Your house is closed for repairs | Your house has a throne and royal guards | *Your house is very fine and splendid | Your house is made of stone
exercise: «The picture palace was full of families on a rainy Saturday.» What kind of place is a picture palace? | A bishop's office | *A big, splendid building for public fun | A king's home with guards | A cold church on a hill
test: «At the bottom are the facts: dates, official statements from the Palace, things filmed for everyone to see.» What does Palace most likely mean here? | A bishop's shed | A small cottage | A cinema | *The royal family's office
test: Which phrase names a famous royal home in London? | Ice palace | Royal guard | Picture palace | *Buckingham Palace
exam: Which sentence uses palace informally, to mean a very fine house? | «At the bottom are the facts from the Palace.» | «The Butterfly stamps, and the Djinns jerk the Palace into the air.» | *«Your new house is a palace!» | «The Assyrians built great palaces at Nineveh.»
